"quoi": "Half of the crack users on Zurich's open drug scene come from other municipalities or cantons. In the autumn of 2025, the City opened a consumption facility reserved for \"outsiders\", with the stated aim of redirecting them towards their home municipality (\"Rückvermittlung\"). One year later, the Tages-Anzeiger concludes that the redirection is failing: outside Zurich there is no provision at all, neither an inhalation room in the east of the canton nor a consumption facility in Winterthur, and the political pressure is absent wherever the scenes are not visible. In February, the City recorded some twenty first contacts with municipalities and no actual returns. Police checks and removals have no lasting effect: \"they come back\" (NZZ). The cantonal conference of social affairs directors is looking for an overall solution.",
"resultats": "\"One year later, nothing to show\" (Tages-Anzeiger, standfirst and summary); around 20 contacts and 0 returns in the first quarter of 2026 (same outlet, February). No new figure published by the City.",
"theme": "Health — drugs",
"titre": "Sending crack users back to their home municipalities does not work: negative one-year assessment, for want of provision in the periphery",
"transposabilite": "Brussels concentrates users coming from Wallonia and Flanders around Gate and the Ribaucourt district, and the debate on \"dispersal\" or sending users back recurs regularly. The Zurich experience shows that sending people back without reception provision in the periphery produces nothing. The useful avenue is an inter-regional co-financing mechanism for harm reduction facilities and the establishment of decentralised branches, which falls under cooperation agreements between federated entities. Strong transferability, as a counter-example.",
